The required maximum is $3/4$ and is achieved if and only if the $x_i$ are all equal to $1/4$. To prove this, use the binomial expansion of $(\sqrt{x_i} - \sqrt{x_j})^4$ to write
$$
4(x_i + x_j)\sqrt{x_i x_j} = x_i^2 + 6x_i x_j + x_j^2 - (\sqrt{x_i} - \sqrt{x_j})^4,
$$

\begin{aligned}
4 \sum_{1 \le i < j \le 4} (x_i + x_j) \sqrt{x_i x_j} &= 3(x_1 + x_2 + x_3 + x_4)^2 - \sum_{1 \le i < j \le 4} (\sqrt{x_i} - \sqrt{x_j})^4 \\
&\le 3(x_1 + x_2 + x_3 + x_4)^2 = 3;
\end{aligned}
$$
clearly, equality holds if and only if the $x_i$ are all equal, and the constraint forces them all equal to $1/4$.
